<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "https://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Entitlements for the daemon, Contents/MacOS/sage-voiced.

  Separate from SageVoiceBridge.entitlements on purpose. That file's own rule is
  that an entitlement handed to a process which cannot use it is just a larger
  thing to attack, and the daemon has no use for the microphone: it is a
  background launchd job, it never opens an audio input device, and voice
  reaches it as files over Signal. So it gets exactly one key, and not the GUI's.

  ## Why this file exists at all: the calendar mirror never worked

  `sage-voiced` was signed with the hardened runtime and NO entitlements. The
  reason written in scripts/package-app.sh was "it never opens an audio device"
  — true, and it answered the wrong question. Under the hardened runtime a
  process without com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars is refused
  calendar access **silently**:

      EKEventStore.requestFullAccessToEvents -> granted = false
      EKEventStore.authorizationStatus(for: .event) -> notDetermined, still

  No dialog is shown, no error is returned, and the status never leaves
  `notDetermined` — so `EventKitCalendar.wasRefused` is false and every tick
  asks again, for ever. bridge.log on the owner's Mac carried 177 consecutive
  "calendar access was declined" lines, one every sixteen minutes, from 4 August
  2026 onward, and not one successful write.

  Worse than a refusal, because a refusal can be undone: with no entitlement the
  process cannot appear in System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Calendars at
  all, so there is nothing for the owner to switch on. The dead end had no door.

  ## Measured, not reasoned — and then measured again, because the first
  ## conclusion was wrong

  Nine variants were run under launchd and in the GUI on 6 August 2026, macOS 26
  (Darwin 25.3.0), and this file shipped in 1.7.6 carrying their verdict: that
  the embedded Info.plist, the CFBundleIdentifier and the codesign identifier all
  changed nothing, and that adding this one key to the daemon was the whole fix.

  **That verdict was wrong, and 1.7.7 proved it in production.** With this key
  present on a Developer ID, hardened-runtime `sage-voiced`, on a Mac whose TCC
  record had been reset, bridge.log still read:

      2026-08-06 09:47:06 [calendar] macOS refused calendar access without
      asking, and left the status at "not determined".

  The flaw in the nine variants was that every *granting* one put the entitlement
  on the executable TCC actually evaluates, and no variant isolated the case this
  bundle is in. Four fresh variants on 6 August 2026, each a Developer ID,
  hardened-runtime LaunchAgent under a bundle identifier that had never been
  asked, differing only in which binary carried the key:

      main executable   nested helper   result
      entitled          (is main)       prompt shown -> granted=true, fullAccess
      NOT entitled      entitled        NO PROMPT, granted=false, notDetermined
      entitled          entitled        prompt shown
      entitled          NOT entitled    prompt shown

  Row two is Mynah 1.7.7 exactly, and it reproduces the owner's symptom on the
  first try. **TCC evaluates the entitlement on the enclosing app bundle's main
  executable — Contents/MacOS/Mynah — not on the nested helper that makes the
  request.** A key on the daemon alone could never have worked, and rows three
  and four say the main executable's key is the load-bearing one.

  So the fix is in SageVoiceBridge.entitlements, not here. This key is kept
  because row three is what ships and is the configuration actually measured, and
  because dropping it would leave the daemon depending on an attribution rule
  Apple has never documented and could tighten. But do not mistake it for the
  thing that makes the mirror work.

  The general rule, which is the part worth carrying to the next TCC feature:
  **a probe only proves something if one of its variants is the shape you
  actually ship.** Nine variants that all differ from production in the same way
  agree with each other and say nothing.

  That is also why the mirror appeared to work: `sage-voiced calendar` run by
  hand from Terminal inherits Terminal's calendar grant, and that is what wrote
  the fifteen events sitting in the owner's Mynah calendar. The daemon has never
  written one.

  ## The paired sentence

  NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription in resources/Info.plist. macOS reads it
  from the bundle this executable lives in, which is why the daemon must stay
  inside Mynah.app rather than being installed beside it. Measured on the same
  day: a helper whose name does NOT match CFBundleExecutable still resolves
  Bundle.main to the enclosing .app and still reads that string, so the name
  mismatch between `sage-voiced` and `Mynah` is not a problem and never was.
-->
<dict>
  <!-- Required. Without it the hardened runtime refuses EventKit before macOS
       ever gets as far as asking the owner, and it does so without saying
       anything. See the long note above; this is one line and it is the whole
       fix for a feature that has never once run in production. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
